+# W385625 Stage 1-C — isolated verification, review handoff and the joined loop
+
+Ready for **independent substantive review**. Canonical Work `2b077949-W385625`; parent W9901. Pinned base `14276917cf4eceaf494dc04fe158aaad2440e7d6` (the accepted Stage 1-A/1-B checkpoint). No native custody claim, no live run, no provider invocation, no Git mutation, no main import, no live instance edit and no output-publication recovery.
+
+## What was asked and what is delivered
+
+One complete supported REC-8 loop, joined end to end:
+
+owner-authorized retained recovery admission (Stage 1-B, unchanged) → **source-aware private materialization of the exact retained base/candidate** → **the approved corrected verifier argv in the approved cwd/env/tool/runtime context** → **an attributable durable result** → **automatic independent substantive review of the SAME retained candidate and its evidence**.
+
+Plus the four endings that are not that: a renewed verification failure (one new failure key, one analysis, one owner item, no successor), that analysis's own finite ending (owner, no recursion), expiry after a passed verification (held owner item, no analysis, no retry) and the owner-authorized review-only recovery it earns.
+
+## Product delta
+
+| Path | Change |
+| --- | --- |
+| `job_manager/retained_verification.py` | **added** — the five acts, the approved execution-context document, the journal, the readiness and the level-triggered pass |
+| `tools/recovery_execution.py` | **added** — the host command runner, the seam a deployment composes, the declared-context producer |
+| `job_manager/retained_recovery.py` | the execution gate OPENS on the committed handoff; `candidate_now` published; the owner read reports the execution half; readiness tells the truth |
+| `job_manager/scheduler.py` | K **M2** — pooled admission refuses a *closed* gate and names its reason |
+| `job_manager/fresh_attempt.py` | K **M3** — `_over` reads a gate's state defensively; the path stays unreachable and a case proves it |
+| `job_manager/documents.py` | one additive closed document, `recovery.verification`; the sweep contract is unchanged |
+| `job_manager/manager.py` | the verification pass on the optional seam |
+| `job_manager/__init__.py` | the Stage 1-C surface |
+| `tools/job_observation.py` | K **M1** — a closed recovery gate is named by its Job and its own reason |
+| `tools/parallel_test.py` | the new module into exactly one registry; the consumed profile definition |
+| `tests/job_manager/test_retained_recovery_execution.py` | **added** — the submitted focused acceptance module |
+
+Exact per-file SHA-256 are in `manifest.json`. **No existing test file was edited**: the bounded test-change scope for this task is none, and the delta adds one new module and nothing else under `tests/`.
+
+## The rulings this slice keeps, and where each one is enforced
+
+- **A passed account alone cannot open review.** `execution_gate` opens on the committed `recovery.candidate-handoff` and on nothing weaker; the handoff exists only after the materialized candidate is re-measured against the bound digest and the reused PASSED verification is bound by identity and digest. A case drives a passed account with no handoff and finds the gate shut and the tick silent.
+- **Verifier output never becomes a candidate.** The candidate root is measured before and after the command; the retained account lives under a separate `output/verification` root; the handoff binds the CUSTODY's candidate identity. One case writes into the materialized candidate tree on purpose and the handoff refuses by digest.
+- **Custody is immutable.** The original custody tree and the whole control store are byte-identical across the complete loop; one case compares both.
+- **No implementation redispatch.** The successor carries one `review` stage; `owned_stages` refuses `implementation` by name; the joined case counts every act the tick asked the deployment for and finds exactly one — `admit recovery-D1/review`.
+- **Intent before effect, and unknown holds.** The approved command runs inside an exclusive window on the private workspace, after a durable `recovery.verification-intent` is journalled. An intent with no account is UNKNOWN: the next attempt refuses `ambiguous/operation`, the pass goes quiet, and no second child process is ever started. Two concurrent passes start exactly one process.
+- **Zero retry, zero correction, exact once-per-successor effects.** Every act is journalled under an identity derived from the successor Job; a restart at each cut resumes at the next act and the child-process count stays at one.
+- **Cancellation and expiry are re-read at every act.** A cancelled successor performs nothing and is never revived; an expired one is held rather than driven, and its old deadline is untouched.
+- **The approved bounds are unchanged.** `stage1-verification-review/1` by identity and digest, 9960 s overall including queue, 4800 s per stage, 900 s per verification command (which is the ceiling the real child process ran under, asserted), 3600 s review provider turn, zero retries and zero corrections. The review-only path reuses the SAME profile with the verification stage omitted. No profile was added, widened or installed.
+- **Context is bound, not implied.** The owner approves a `baton.v12.recovery-verification-context/1` digest covering the working directory, the environment, the tool and the runtime selection; the execution refuses when the deployment's declared context digests to anything else. A case moves each member and watches the digest move.
+- **This manager manufactures no verdict.** The handoff document carries no verdict word; the review stage is queued rather than terminal; a scripted rejection and a scripted acceptance both leave this path's records unchanged.
+
+## K's review notes M1–M3
+
+- **M1** — `tools/job_observation._gate_text` names a recovery gate by its Job and its own closed reason. Candidate cases assert the new text, assert the dependency-gate text is byte-identical to what it always was, and assert the hold disappears once the gate opens.
+- **M2** — two candidate cases drive `PooledManagerOperations.admit` directly: a closed gate refuses and names its reason with nothing allocated; a validated handoff means the gate is no longer what refuses. The scheduler now tests `gate["open"]` rather than the gate's existence, which is what makes the handoff reachable at all.
+- **M3** — `fresh_attempt._over` reads `gate.get("state")`, so the two gate shapes cannot fault it, and a gate with no state is not read as "never opens". A second case proves the path stays unreachable: `fresh_attempt.admit` of a recovery Job refuses the inherited profile before any effect.
+- **N1** (Stage 1-B review) — the post-verification hold is this slice's: `recovery.post-verification-hold`, delivered once, preserving the passed verification and carrying the three lineage operands a review-only decision must name back. The owner reads it through the installed `recovery-item`.
+
+## The joined acceptance, and what is scripted
+
+`TheJoinedAcceptanceRunsTheShippedOwnersOnABootstrappedInstance` installs a real disposable instance with `tools.bootstrap`, builds the retained verification failure into **that instance's own** Job store and control store, and runs the installed owner commands **with no `--owners` operand at all** — so the five owner ports are `tools.recovery_decision.owners`', composed over the instance's own persisted identity, its own emitted configuration and its own Authority. The owner is authorized by the real `approve` grant `bootstrap` makes in the Work's own scope; the deployment's own selector names the reviewer it configured; the host evidence is this instance's own, with the workspace token really acquired, launched, bound and **returned** through the resource owner's own operations.
+
+Honestly labelled:
+
+- the **analysis** diagnosis is a SCRIPTED document. It demonstrates orchestration and evidence fidelity, not live diagnostic quality.
+- the **reviewer** is not run. What is proved is that the ordinary tick admits the configured independent review of the same candidate automatically once the handoff is validated, and that this manager writes no verdict. The accept and the reject in the last class are SCRIPTED.
+- the **verifier is real**: a real `subprocess.run` of the exact approved argv, in the materialized candidate's own directory, with exactly the declared environment, under the approved 900-second ceiling. Its five structural predicates are a STRUCTURAL check and certify nothing about content — which is why the independent review afterwards is the thing that judges substance.
+
+Also in the joined case: the prior Authority assignment is ended by **its own supported operation** (`end`) and a **fresh claim** is then taken on the **reused** Work — a new generation, by its own session, with no impersonation and no takeover. Host settlement alone does not end it, and the case asserts that order. A second case removes exactly one host fact (the cleanup the host still owes) and finds the shipped port refusing, with the owner's decision recorded and the admission not performed — both facts, not one.
+
+## Measured evidence
+
+See `COUNTS.md` for per-case counts, failures, skips and measured durations, and the retained logs beside it. The focused acceptance is the command this Work's verification runs:
+
+```
+bash -c 'set -euo pipefail; mkdir -p work/scratch/w385625-verification; export BATON_V12_DISK_ROOT="$PWD/work/scratch/w385625-verification"; cd v12/python; export PYTHONPATH=src; exec python3 -m unittest -v tests.job_manager.test_retained_recovery_execution tests.job_manager.test_retained_recovery_admission'
+```
+
+## Limitations, stated rather than implied
+
+1. **The reviewer's own runtime is not exercised.** No container, no image, no provider and no daemon is reached anywhere in this delta. What is proved is the manager-side release and the same-candidate delivery; the reviewer's investigation is the reviewer's.
+2. **A second recovery round is the owner's next explicit act and is not admitted here.** A renewed verification failure gets its own failure key, one analysis intent under the owner-approved `stage1-analysis/1` bounds and one owner item. Stage 1-B's per-failure fence then refuses a second approval of the ORIGINAL failure in its own words — "a further round is a new owner decision on NEW EVIDENCE" — and this slice delivers that new evidence. Admitting a successor against a renewed key through the `recovery-item`/`recovery-decide` surface is **not** implemented: those two verbs resolve a `failure_analysis` failure record, and a manager-executed verification has no frozen worker attempt for `record_failure` to classify. A reviewer should classify whether that surface extension belongs to this outcome or to a later one; nothing here loops automatically either way.
+3. **The renewed analysis is not dispatched by this slice.** Its intent, role, profile, digest and admitted deadlines are journalled; ordering the turn belongs to the analysis seam Stage 1-A owns, and a manager-executed verification leaves no control-store attempt for that seam to drive.
+4. **A command the host could not start leaves the intent standing and holds.** The runner's typed refusal is reported as the condition it is, and the next attempt refuses `ambiguous/operation` rather than starting a second process. This is conservative on purpose — this manager did not watch the child and will not decide from a refusal that nothing ran — and the way forward is the owner's explicit disposition.
+5. **The sweep document does not name this pass.** The `sweep` contract's member set is closed and this build does not widen it; what the pass did is visible in the five durable records, the owner items and the review stage's own gate. A caller that wants the per-act list drives `advance_verification` and is handed it.
+6. **Three job_manager path-message cases, one `test_native_permission` case and two authority bounded-text cases fail identically on the pinned base** in this environment and are not this delta's. `baseline-environment-failures.log` and `candidate-environment-failures.log` hold both runs and their failing ids are identical.
+7. **The bootstrapped-instance fixtures need real storage outside the checkout.** `tests.tools.test_stack._disk_root_outside_the_checkout` refuses rather than silently falling back, which is correct and is exactly this verifier's environment: its own layout record puts `HOME`, `TMPDIR` and the cache on tmpfs and `/var/tmp` is read-only. So the new module names `BATON_V12_STACK_TEST_ROOT` at import with `setdefault` — an operator's own choice always wins — after proving a candidate against the same two product rules the accepted chooser applies. Without that, `test_retained_recovery_admission`'s own installed-instance case fails here for the environment, which is the condition W385623's manifest already recorded. No test file was changed.
+8. **Development limits are separate from product bounds.** This Work's own execution used `native-handoff-heavy/1` (7200/9000/21600/900) and the 100 MiB proposal cap; those are not REC-8 recovery bounds and nothing here changes the approved 9960/4800/3600/900.
+
+## Exact candidate identity
+
+The candidate is the filesystem bytes named, path by path and digest by digest, in `manifest.json`. That file's own SHA-256 is `166c35c28266a38eced58fd05673a5caa745849ef84ea323f07ca4bfa16d3d91`, which is the one digest that binds this return. `evidence.sha256` beside it covers every other file in this directory; it does not digest itself, for the obvious reason.
+
+## Return
+
+Independent substantive review of the exact digest-bound candidate in `manifest.json`. The owner performs any Git checkpoint and import of approved product bytes; the dossier files here are evidence and must not overwrite later coordinator history. W385694 and W385695 remain separate, and publication-resume stays out of this outcome.
